we've come here today to speak to  christoph hoyskin in berlin for many  years he was angela merkel's foreign  policy advisor he was also germany's  ambassador to the united nations so he's  german one of germany's most senior  diplomats and he's now the chairman of  the prestigious munich security  conference ambassador thanks for joining  dw i want to start by talking about  um the diplomatic efforts that we see  ongoing at the moment between russia and  ukraine we are beginning to see the  possible controls of some kind of  agreement involving neutrality of  ukraine and ukraine receiving security  guarantees from outside guarantors do  you feel this is the kind of agreement  that could bring an end to this war  i have a fundamental problem and that is  that  over the years there have been many  agreements reached with russia  i remind you of the so-called budapest  memorandum in 94 where russia guaranteed  the  sovereignty and territory integrity of  ukraine and ukraine gave up its nuclear  weapons  i  recall the minsk agreement where  which was concluded after last time  russia invaded  ukraine it was an agreement which said  that there should be a ceasefire and  they should withdraw their heavy weapons  and the monetary should be allowed to  actually have  you know their freeway through all of  the dondas and russia didn't observe any  of this so my problem is that to agree  with russia today is very difficult  because russia under putin has lost all  credibility nobody trusts that russia  will actually then implement an  agreement it entered into and yet putin  is in power  this is the person who zelinski is  having to deal with volatilinski the  ukrainian president what choice does he  have but to negotiate no he doesn't have  a choice i just tell you what i think is  the outcome you cannot rely on this of  course zelensky has to he has to see to  it that he gets a ceasefire he has to  see to it that putin stops to bomb  civilians stops to commit  war crimes  now  if vladimir putin does go down this road  with an agreement of this type this  falls well short of the war aims that he  seemed to be professing at the beginning  of this war in those famous speeches he  made it made it sound like he had no  respect for ukraine as a sovereign state  um and that he essentially wanted to  affect regime change there  so what should we understand from the  fact that he seems to be considering an  agreement like this does it mean that he  feels he's losing in ukraine yes i think  that um he sees that what you know he  built up in his fantasy about a ukraine  that is very pro-russian where you have  a saudi government there and and and  there is no um ukrainian identity he  realizes that everything he thought  ukraine would be is is not it's not true  but um so he's on the defensive so i  think he has a certain interest in maybe  um  coming to a to a stop because they are  losing hundreds or thousands of soldiers  but  we must not forget you cannot trust  putin so he may then agree to something  but then he will regroup and try again  to to achieve you have to understand  what is real game is what is this he is  afraid that in russia something happens  that has happened in other countries in  the neighborhood that is that countries  that that the population is sick and  tired of this post-soviet oligarchic  society and he's afraid that this may  happen in russia as well so therefore he  has to stop all this and for him  a successful ukraine a ukraine that has  a prosperous industry a prosperous  economy where you have democracy and all  this this may be a nightmare for him  because people in in russia will ask how  about us and therefore he cannot allow  ukraine to succeed and now he sees that  all what is what he has been aiming for  is is crumbling  how weakened is putin i mean there's  been some speculation about whether  there could be a sort of palace coup  against him in russia do you think that  is something that is a realistic  prospect at the moment i cannot um you  know i have no idea how how this is  everybody says well you know in russia  this was under the czars and under  stalin you know they could do with their  population what they wanted to so there  is no danger for the ruler on the other  hand we see  you know on russian television we see in  the main news there is somebody standing  up for the journalists and then and this  sees that people are become much more  courageous it's wonderful what you have  from these young people that they risk  like navalny you know they risk going to  prison because they want another russia  so i think putin is in in serious  problems how serious i cannot tell you  how concerned are you i mean there's a  speculation also that putin goes in the  opposite direction he doesn't sign an  agreement he doesn't back down but he  doubles down he escalates his way  through this  how biggest concern is that for you  you you have to put yourself into  his shoes he sees that what he wanted to  achieve  was not able to he was not able to  achieve  and  he doesn't have any respect for  human rights for humanitarian law you  have seen what putin has done to the  citizen of grozny you have seen what  putin has done to the citizens of aleppo  so there may be a danger that he is  ready to double down yes and if he goes  ahead and does that what is the proper  response in your view from the west so  far the west has been remarkably united  in terms of imposing very tough  sanctions but it has stopped short of of  sanctions at least the europeans have on  energy  um is that a card that you feel that  germany and the rest of europe might at  some point be forced to draw  if putin seriously escalates  i think there has been really a  remarkable united front of all the  governments um and i this is not only  you know europe and the u.s you have  this worldwide  alliance of of countries the votes at  the u.n were overwhelming putin today is  supported is supported by kim jong-un by  assad and lukashenko can you imagine so  this is something which is really  remarkable  i've seen i'm i'm really  surprised by the amount of solidarity we  have in east european countries in my  own countries people want to support  ukraine that they see what putin is  doing  and of course to to go down in the  direction now of an energy of a gas  embargo is something very tough it has  serious it has serious repercussions but  um you know right now there are plans to  um also by the commission to  um see to it that we get faster to um an  independence of russian energy and i  think people are ready to um to go ahead  i think that people will not allow um  russia to do with kiev uh what he has  done to aleppo and what he has done also  to grosseni there are also more and more  quite serious voices  coming in and advocating  some  degree of intervention by nato whether  it is in trying to  create a so-called no-fly zone over  ukraine uh or there was a proposal um  from a polish side  on this trip to kiev  to create what they described as a nato  peacekeeping force could there come a  point where one of those options becomes  a more serious one i think this is  something the alliance has to discuss so  far  it is clear that nato as an organization  will not be involved this is in the  logic of the atlantic treaty this is why  also in in 2008 the basic decision was  taken that the ukraine and georgia that  was georgia at the time in the center of  discussion will  not get a membership and not become  member of nato because of the risk of a  direct confrontation of a third world  war so  i think we will not go that way not to  risk a world war but  everything that is possible below that  level i think one should discuss and see  how um how it is implemented and how  close are we getting to maybe even an  accidental clash of some kind we saw a  surveillance drone reportedly landing uh  in romania  um  we've got a lot of concern in poland and  other eastern nato flank countries about  what could happen  well this is something we have  we have lived through also during the  cold war um  russia and the united states the warsaw  pact and and nato  doing all these many years of of severe  political crisis were able to manage it  in a way that there was no that direct  confrontation and i'm  um actually pretty optimistic that this  doesn't happen  and if there are accidents that they  will be managed in a way that there is  no escalation i want to take a step back  at this point just to look at the bigger  global picture and particularly china  the americans are saying that russia has  requested support and that china is  already providing economic and financial  support to the russians  and  the americans saying that they are  considering providing military support  what happens if china does go down that  road if china starts quite actively  supporting russia does this then become  a proxy war between china and the west  well china has to make a choice um  china has been um also in the u.n where  it worked and everywhere they say and  and underline um you know the importance  of national sovereignty know when we  criticize china about what they do with  hong kong what they do with the urger  say you have nothing to do with it this  is national sovereignty  and now there is their partner russia  invading ukraine and violating national  sovereignty so i think that they have  they are at pain to to um you know  somehow um not support but tolerate it  be be kind to the russians so they are  in a real problem and they know if they  now start actively to support russia  that they are they lose their standing  as a country that is trying to have in  the world you know kind of an  alternative power country that stands  also for um you know  new power in the united nations that  respect sovereignty and  serves as kind of a model for other  countries if they go they know if they  go that way  they will lose credibility to a degree  that they they are not ready to accept  because then they are also  being siding with the paria state so i  have my doubt on the other hand of  course they they don't want to put in  and and and see are close so  c doesn't want to let putin down but he  knows that putin may drag him into  something which then has serious  repercussion because i'm sure that the  us and others are watching very  carefully and does he stay neutral or  does he get involved in an unacceptable  way on the side of russia  and what would be the appropriate  response for the west but also  particularly europe which has always  been walking it to some extent its own  line towards china  if china does make that move  what's the appropriate response for  europe i think we have to get  away from this notion of the west you  know we have to go to new york we have  to say this is international law what  russia is doing is violating  international law and if china then also  openly supports  russia in this i think we have to bring  it to the united nations say this is um  a country that is now not respecting  international law anymore but supporting  a country that is um um viral is is a  war basically a war criminal and  committing war crimes and i think we  should take it take it to the to the u.n  but of course also discuss it between  transatlantic partners but this goes  beyond you know what we are witnessing  here right now what russia is doing is  the violation of the very foundation of  the order that was established after the  second world war it's a violation of the  u.n charter it's a violation of the um  universal declaration of human rights so  this is nothing between west and east  this is actually an attack on the global  international order  christoph heisken thank you very much  for taking time to speak to us today  it's my pleasure thank you

